So the strategy works. Let me also double-check the DD lemma once more (it's the crux): For a non-Random god, DD(P) is answered "da" ⇔ P, regardless of da/ja meaning.

DD(P) = "If I asked you 'P?', would you say 'da'?"

God T: When asked P, says da-word (yes-word) iff P. So "would you say da" is true ⇔ (P and da=yes) or (not P and da=no). Answer "da" ⇔ (that's true and da=yes) or (that's false and da=no) ⇔ (P ⇔ da=yes) ⇔ P. ✓ (Case: da=yes: "da" ⇔ P ✓. da=no: "da" ⇔ (not P) is false... let me redo: da=no. When asked P: says yes-word iff P. "would you say da" ⇔ (not P). Answer: says da (no-word) ⇔ (not P) is false ⇔ P. ✓)
God F: When asked P: says yes-word iff not P. "would you say da" ⇔ (P and da=no) or (not P and da=yes) = (P ⇔ da=no). Answer: God F says da iff (that) is false... God F says da (word w) iff: if w=yes-word: iff (that) is false; if w=no-word: iff (that) is true. Case da=yes: "da" ⇔ not[(P and da=no) or (not P and da=yes)] = not[not P] = P... let me compute: that = (P ⇔ da=no) = (P ⇔ false) = not P. God F says da (yes) ⇔ not(that) = P. ✓. Case da=no: that = (P ⇔ da=no) = P. God F says da (no-word) ⇔ that = P. ✓.
So DD lemma verified: Non-Random gods answer "da" ⇔ P.

Now, there's a subtle point about Random: "Random's answer is completely random: each time, he flips a coin in his head to decide whether to lie or tell the truth." So Random answers DD(P): coin: Truth → "da" ⇔ P; Lie → "da" ⇔ not P. Either way, "da" with probability 1/2 (assuming P is fixed). In my strategy, Random's answer is treated as arbitrary (either branch reachable) — correct since the coin could go either way, and for correctness we need the strategy to work for all coin outcomes. ✓

One more subtlety: When Random "lies", does he say the opposite of the truth? Yes: "flips a coin to decide whether to lie or tell the truth." So his raw answer to a question is "yes" or "no" (his language) — coin heads: truthful answer, tails: lie. Either way, the word is either "da" or "ja", 50/50. ✓ In my analysis, Random's DD answer can be either word — correct.

Another subtlety: In my strategy, Q3 in branches 3/4 asks A. And Q2 in branch Q1=ja asks A, so we ask A twice (Q2, Q3) in branches 3/4. That's allowed ("you may ask the same god multiple questions"). ✓

