Here's the complete solution — verified branch by branch, so you can trust every answer.

The tool: the "da-ified" question

Define DD(P) = "If I asked you 'P?', would you say 'da'?"

Claim: For a True or False god, the answer is "da" iff P is true — regardless of which word means yes. (Proof table: 4 cases, each da⇔P.) This cancels two confusions at once: the god's lie (one flip) and the da/ja swap (the second flip). Two flips = no flip.

Caveat: For Random, DD gives a coin flip — so we must ensure that whenever we interpret an answer, the respondent is known to be non-Random.

The questions

Q1 (to B): DD("Is A Random?")
Q2: If Q1=da → to C: DD("Is B Random?"). If Q1=ja → to A: DD("Is B Random?").
Q3: If Q2=da → to A: DD("Are you True?").
If Q2=ja → if Q1=da: to B: DD("Are you True?"); if Q1=ja: to A: DD("Are you True?").

Why it works

After Q1, the possible states (roles of A, B, C ∈ T, F, R):

Q1=da: (R,T,F), (R,F,T) [B non-Random, A=R] or (T,R,F), (F,R,T) [B=R].
Q1=ja: (T,F,R), (F,T,R) [C=R] or (T,R,F), (F,R,T) [B=R].
Key: In every state where Q1=da, C is non-Random; in every state where Q1=ja, A is non-Random. So Q2's respondent is never Random → Q2 is a reliable bit:

Q2=da ⇔ B is Random. (If Q1=ja: s3-s6: A non-Random ✓.)
Q2=ja ⇔ B is not Random → then Q1=da → A=R; Q1=ja → C=R.
So after Q2, we know exactly who is Random:

Q2=da → B=R. Q2=ja, Q1=da → A=R. Q2=ja, Q1=ja → C=R.
And in each case, the god we ask Q3 is non-Random, so Q3=da ⇔ "that god is True":

B=R case: Ask A → da: A=T, ja: A=F.
A=R case: Ask B → da: B=T, ja: B=F.
C=R case: Ask A → da: A=T, ja: A=F.
Q3 resolves the last 50/50. The third god gets the remaining role.

Decoding table (Q1, Q2, Q3 → identity):
| Answers | Result |
| da,da,da | A=T, B=R, C=F |
| ja,da,da | A=T, B=R, C=F |
| da,da,ja | A=F, B=R, C=T |
| ja,da,ja | A=F, B=R, C=T |
| da,ja,da | A=R, B=T, C=F |
| da,ja,ja | A=R, B=F, C=T |
| ja,ja,da | A=T, B=F, C=R |
| ja,ja,ja | A=F, B=T, C=R |

Note that only Q1 can come from Random; Q2 and Q3 are asked to gods guaranteed non-Random, so their bits are trustworthy. Random's coin only decides which column we're in.

